\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 My take:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 TikTok:
\f0\b0 \'a0real voice + occasional face wins.\cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cb3 Use your face for the hook or the last 2 seconds, then mostly show the app. Example: quick face clip saying, \'93If your notes live in random text files, try this,\'94 then screen recording. TikTok tends to reward authenticity and completion, and a real founder voice usually feels less ad-like than AI.\

\f1\b Instagram Reels:
\f0\b0 \'a0real voice or captions-first.\cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cb3 IG often gets watched muted, so your video should work with text overlays even if nobody hears you. Face helps trust, but app b-roll with your voiceover is probably the best balance. Don\'92t make it too polished.\

\f1\b Bluesky:
\f0\b0 \'a0least \'93ad voice,\'94 most human.\cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cb3 Post the video with a conversational caption, maybe even \'93I made a tiny demo showing note links/backlinks for people who just use plain text notes.\'94 Bluesky is more discussion/community-driven; showing that you\'92re the person building it matters more than production gloss.\
I\'92d avoid AI voiceover for the first batch. It can work for anonymous meme/product accounts, but for a solo indie tool it slightly weakens the best story: a real person made this because the workflow annoyed them.\
